# Cloudflare Pages reads this file and applies the headers to matching paths.
#
# The `noindex` meta tag in index.astro is the primary signal; this covers what
# a meta tag cannot. `og.png` and every cover are images, with no HTML to carry
# a tag, and an image indexed on its own is a book cover captioned with a title.
#
# `X-Robots-Tag` does not affect link-preview scrapers — Slack, iMessage and the
# rest are not search engines and do not consult it — so the preview still works.

# ⚠️ The Content-Security-Policy is NOT here, and this file used to imply it was.
#
# The comment that stood here said the shelf "has no scripts from anywhere else"
# and that "saying so costs nothing" — describing a header that was never added.
# It was not wrong about the shelf's behaviour; it was wrong about the file it
# was in, and it is the reason the absence went unnoticed, because a reader of
# `_headers` was told the mitigation was present. See issue #127.
#
# Astro now generates the policy per page as a `<meta http-equiv>`, with
# `script-src` and `style-src` hashes computed from what each page actually
# contains — see the `security.csp` block in `packages/site/astro.config.mjs`
# for why that beats a hand-written copy here.
#
# `gates/public-build-artifact.test.ts` (G20) holds every built page to the whole
# directive set, by name and by source, hashes excluded. Saying so is only worth
# anything because it is now true — the first version of that rule pinned two
# directives and left four deletable, which is the sentence above happening again
# one paragraph down.
#
# `frame-ancestors` is the one directive a `<meta>` CSP cannot carry — browsers
# ignore it there — so it is below, as a policy of exactly one directive.
#
# That is **not** a second copy of the page's policy, and the distinction is the
# point: the two are disjoint, so neither can drift from the other. A hand-written
# copy of the whole policy would go stale (see astro.config.mjs); a directive the
# generated one structurally cannot contain has nowhere else to live. Policies
# compose — every one is enforced — and one declaring no fetch directives
# restricts nothing except framing.
#
# `X-Frame-Options: DENY` stays beside it rather than being replaced by it. For
# `DENY` the two are equivalent in every browser that matters; the exception worth
# the line is that `frame-ancestors` covers `<embed>` and `<object>` uniformly
# where `X-Frame-Options` historically did not, and `X-Frame-Options` is read by
# clients predating CSP framing. The rule asserts both, so neither can be deleted
# on the theory that the other covers it.
#
# This block is keyed `/*`, not `/`. `Referrer-Policy` and `X-Content-Type-Options`
# were on the root page alone while the site had one page; `/attribution` is the
# second, and it was answering Cloudflare's default
# `strict-origin-when-cross-origin` — the same widening the `robots` rule in
# `scripts/lib/public-build.ts` already went through, for the same reason.
/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Frame-Options: DENY

# Images must expire on the same schedule as the index that describes them.
#
# Cloudflare Pages serves HTML and JSON with `max-age=0, must-revalidate` and
# images with `max-age=14400` — four hours. Every filename here is a slug of a
# book title and is *rewritten in place* by each deploy, so those two defaults
# put the site into a state where `library.json` is current and the covers it
# points at are up to four hours old.
#
# That is not merely stale, it is incoherent: `coverAspect` in library.json is
# measured from the file this build staged, and the shelf draws each cover at
# that ratio. Serve new metadata against old bytes and books render at the wrong
# shape. The same skew shipped the fix for the mobile crash to an origin nobody
# could see — the deploy succeeded, the phones kept crashing, and nothing said
# so.
#
# The honest fix is for the pieces of one build to expire together. ETag makes
# that cheap: a cover that has not changed costs a 304, not a re-download.
/covers/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/og.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
